n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") < \"1.0\"\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") > \"1.0b2\"\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") == \"1.0a1\"\n```\nThese cover 170-171 plus downstream branches.\n\nNow, imports: The module is `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py`. Test import: `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import StrictVersion`. Should I guard the import? The task says write test functions; typical convention in these tasks is a plain import at top. I'll do a straightforward import. Maybe add a module-level import with pytest.importorskip? The instructions say minimal top-level code; a plain import is fine.\n\nCleanup/state pollution: These tests don't modify global state \u2014 no monkeypatching needed except maybe for the flippy class which is local to the test. The flippy subclass is defined inside the test function to avoid polluting module namespace. Good.\n\nOne concern: `StrictVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"` uses `Version.__eq__` which calls `_cmp`; `_cmp` returns 0; `0 == 0` \u2192 True. But also `\"1.0\".__eq__(StrictVersion(...))`? Only if left operand's `__eq__` returns NotImplemented. Left is StrictVersion \u2192 returns True. Fine.\n\nFor the `NotImplemented` test: `v._cmp(object())` \u2014 line 170: isinstance(object(), str)? No. Line 172: `elif not isinstance(other, StrictVersion)` \u2192 True \u2192 return NotImplemented. \u2713 Covers 172-173.\n\nEdge: also test that rich comparison with unsupported type raises TypeError (since Python converts NotImplemented from both sides):\n```python\nwith pytest.raises(TypeError):\n    v < 42\n```\nThat's a nice postcondition check. `v < 42`: `Version.__lt__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 Python tries `42.__gt__(v)` \u2192 NotImplemented \u2192 TypeError. \u2713\n\nNow the flippy test in detail:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_unreachable_else_raises_assertion_error():\n    class FlippyPrerelease(StrictVersion):\n        \"\"\"StrictVersion whose ``prerelease`` alternates truthiness on every read,\n        making all four prerelease branches false so the defensive else runs.\"\"\"\n\n        def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n            super().__init__(vstring)\n            self._flip = False\n\n        @property\n        def prerelease(self):\n            self._flip = not self._flip\n            return self._flip\n\n        @prerelease.setter\n        def prerelease(self, value):\n            # absorb assignments made by parse(); behavior comes from the getter\n            self._last_assigned = value\n\n    flippy = FlippyPrerelease(\"1.0\")\n    plain = StrictVersion(\"1.0\")\n\n    # sanity: versions match so comparison proceeds to the prerelease ladder\n    assert flippy.version == plain.version\n\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"never get here\"):\n        flippy._cmp(plain)\n```\n\nTrace again very carefully with real code:\n\n`flippy._cmp(plain)`:\n- line 170: isinstance(plain, str)? No.\n- line 172: not isinstance(plain, StrictVersion)? plain IS StrictVersion \u2192 False \u2192 continue.\n- line 175: `self.version != other.version` \u2192 flippy.version: parse set version=(1,0,0); plain.version=(1,0,0) \u2192 equal \u2192 False \u2192 skip block.\n- line 189: `if (not self.prerelease and not other.prerelease):`\n  - `self.prerelease` \u2192 getter: _flip False\u2192True, return True. `not True` = False \u2192 short-circuit \u2192 cond1 = False.\n- line 191: `elif (self.prerelease and not other.prerelease):`\n  - `self.prerelease` \u2192 _flip True\u2192False, return False \u2192 cond2 = False (short-circuit).\n- line 193: `elif (not self.prerelease and other.prerelease):`\n  - `self.prerelease` \u2192 _flip False\u2192True, return True \u2192 `not True` = False \u2192 cond3 = False (short-circuit).\n- line 195: `elif (self.prerelease and other.prerelease):`\n  - `self.prerelease` \u2192 _flip True\u2192False, return False \u2192 cond4 = False.\n- else (line 202-203): `raise AssertionError(\"never get here\")` \u2713\n\nThe match=\"never get