reate an object of a class that mimics StrictVersion but isn't (for the NotImplemented branch)... no, that's for line 173.\n\n   Another way to hit line 203: make `self.prerelease` a custom object whose `__bool__` alternates. Same idea.\n\n   OK, let me also double check: does `_cmp` get called with `self` being the flippy instance? Yes, `flippy._cmp(other)` directly.\n\n   But hold on \u2014 there's a subtlety: `Version.__eq__` etc. call `self._cmp(other)`. If we call `flippy == other`, `__eq__` calls `_cmp`, gets AssertionError propagates. Either way fine. I'll call `_cmp` directly for clarity, or via operator. Direct call is fine and precise.\n\nNow the other tests:\n\nTest 1: string coercion (lines 170-171):\n```python\ndef test_cmp_with_string():\n    assert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"\n    assert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") < \"1.1\"\n    assert StrictVersion(\"2.0\") > \"1.9\"\n```\nWait, `StrictVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"` \u2192 `__eq__` \u2192 `_cmp(\"1.0\")` \u2192 isinstance str \u2192 other = StrictVersion(\"1.0\") \u2192 versions equal, no prereleases \u2192 return 0 \u2192 `0 == 0` \u2192 True. \u2713 Lines 170, 171 covered.\n\nAlso test comparing with a string where versions differ to cover 178/179 via string path? Not needed separately but fine.\n\nTest 2: NotImplemented (lines 172-173):\n```python\ndef test_cmp_with_unsupported_type_returns_not_implemented():\n    v = StrictVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert v._cmp(object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert v._cmp(42) is NotImplemented\n    assert (v == 42) is False  # __eq__ converts NotImplemented to False\n```\nNote: `v == 42` \u2192 `Version.__eq__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 Python falls back to reflected `int.__eq__(42, v)` \u2192 NotImplemented \u2192 identity comparison \u2192 False. So `(v == 42) is False` holds. But safer to assert `v._cmp(42) is NotImplemented` directly. Also `v < 42` would raise TypeError. Keep it simple: assert `_cmp(...) is NotImplemented`.\n\nHmm, but wait: `isinstance(other, str)` \u2014 bool is not str. What about bytes? Not str. OK.\n\nTest 3: version mismatch, self < other (lines 175, 178, 179):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"2.0\")) == -1\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") < StrictVersion(\"2.0\")\n```\n\nTest 4: version mismatch, self > other (lines 175, 180-181 / else branch):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"2.0\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0\")) == 1\nassert StrictVersion(\"2.0\") > StrictVersion(\"1.0\")\n```\n\nTest 5: neither prerelease (189-190):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0.0\")) == 0\n```\nNote \"1.0\" parses to version (1,0,0), prerelease None. \"1.0.0\" \u2192 (1,0,0), None. Equal \u2192 0. \u2713\n\nTest 6: self prerelease, other not (191-192):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0\")) == -1\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") < StrictVersion(\"1.0\")\n```\n\nTest 7: self no prerelease, other has (193-194):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0b2\")) == 1\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") > StrictVersion(\"1.0b2\")\n```\n\nTest 8: both prerelease equal (195-197):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")) == 0\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") == StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")\n```\n\nTest 9: both prerelease, self < other (198-199):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0b1\")) == -1\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") < StrictVersion(\"1.0b1\")\n```\nprerelease tuples: ('a', 1) < ('b', 1) \u2192 True \u2192 -1. \u2713\n\nTest 10: both prerelease, self > other (200-201):\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0b2\")._cmp(StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")) == 1\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0b2\") > StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\")\n```\n('b',2) vs ('a',1): 'b' > 'a' \u2192 1. \u2713\n\nTest 11: AssertionError branch (202-203) via flippy property.\n\nAlso maybe test string coercion combined with prerelease comparisons to ensure line 171 path feeds into later lines:\n```python\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") < \"1.0\"\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0\") > \"1.0b2\"\nassert StrictVersion(\"1.0a1\") == \"1.0a1\"\n```\nThese cover 170-171 plus downstream branches.\n\nNow, imports: The module is `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py`. Test import: `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import StrictVersion`. Should I guard the import? The task says write test functions; typical convention in these tasks is a plain import at top. I'll do a straightforward import. Maybe add a module-level import with pytest.importorskip? The instructions say minimal top-level code; a plain import is fine.\n\nCleanup/state pollution: These tests don't modify global state \u2014 no monkeypatching needed except maybe for the flippy class which is local to the test. The flippy subclass is defined inside the test function to avoid polluting module namespace. Good.\n\nOne concern: `StrictVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"` uses `Version.__eq__` which calls `_cmp`; `_cmp` returns 0; `0 == 0` \u2192 True. But also `\"1.0\".__eq__(StrictVersion(...))`? Only if left operand's `__eq__` returns NotImplemented. Left is StrictVersion \u2192 returns True. Fine.\n\nFor the `NotImplemented` test: `v._cmp(object())` \u2014 line 170: isinstance(object(), str)? No. Line 172: `elif not isinstance(other, StrictVersion)` \u2192 True \u2192 return NotImplemented. \u2713 Covers 172-173.\n\nEdge: also test that rich comparison with unsupported type raises TypeError (since Python converts NotImplemented from both sides):\n```python\nwith pytest.raises(TypeError):\n    v < 42\n```\nThat's a nice postcondition check. `v < 42`: `Version.__lt__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 Python tries `42.__gt__(v)` \u2192 NotImplemented \u2192 TypeError. \u2713\n\nNow the flippy test in detail:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_unreachable_else_raises_assertion_error():\n    class FlippyPrerelease(StrictVersion):\n        \"\"\"StrictVersion whose ``prerelease`` alternates truthiness on every read,\n        maki