assert v.version == [1, 2, 3]\n    assert repr(v) == \"LooseVersion ('1.2.3')\"\n```\n\nAlso cover the false branch: `LooseVersion()` with no args \u2192 `if vstring:` false. And `Version()` base with no args.\n\nTest 2: equality (54-57), including NotImplemented branch.\n\nTest 3: ordering ops.\n\nFor NotImplemented: need `_cmp` returning NotImplemented. With LooseVersion, `_cmp` never returns NotImplemented. So either:\n\n(a) Use a test-local subclass overriding `_cmp`.\n(b) Monkeypatch.\n\nHmm, but wait: if I define a test-local subclass of `Version` (the class under test), the rich comparison methods executed are still `Version.__eq__` etc. (inherited). That covers the target lines. And `Version.__init__` runs with vstring \u2192 line 47-48 covered (calls subclass's parse). And `Version.__repr__` covered via repr(). \n\nBut is defining a local subclass \"testing the unit\" properly? It's a standard technique for abstract base classes. The `Version` class is explicitly an \"Abstract base class\"; testing it requires a concrete subclass. I think combining both approaches is good:\n\n- Use `LooseVersion` (real subclass) for the main-path tests: init with vstring, repr, all five comparisons in both directions, comparing with strings.\n- Use a small local subclass whose `_cmp` returns `NotImplemented` for unsupported types to cover the NotImplemented branches.\n\nAlternatively, monkeypatch `LooseVersion._cmp` to return NotImplemented temporarily. Both fine. A local subclass is cleaner and avoids global patching.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe simpler\u2014define the local subclass with a `_cmp` mirroring StrictVersion:\n\n```python\nclass ComparableVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Concrete Version subclass used to exercise the base-class plumbing.\"\"\"\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.version = tuple(int(part) for part in vstring.split(\".\"))\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \".\".join(str(part) for part in self.version)\n\n    