rue\nassert (a <= b) is True\nassert (b <= a) is False\nassert (a > b) is False\nassert (b > a) is True\nassert (a >= a) is True\nassert (b >= a) is True\nassert (a >= b) is False\n```\n\nAll these go through Version's comparators since DummyVersion doesn't override them. \n\nAlso string operand: `a == \"1.0\"` \u2192 `_cmp` converts \u2192 0 \u2192 True. Covers the `isinstance(other, str)` branch in dummy `_cmp` (though that's our own code, fine).\n\nBranch coverage for `if vstring:` in `Version.__init__`: \n- True: `DummyVersion(\"1.0\")`.\n- False: `DummyVersion()` \u2192 no parse called; assert `not hasattr(v, 'version')`.\n\nNow, about `Version()` directly: `Version()` with no vstring \u2192 `if vstring:` false \u2192 no error. That also covers the false branch using the actual base class. Nice: `Version()` is instantiable as long as we don't pass a vstring. Add assertion: `isinstance(Version(), Version)`. Hmm, that's trivially true; better assert it has no `version` attr: `assert not hasattr(Version(), \"version\")`. Eh, it's fine.\n\nShould I also test `Version(\"1.0\")` raising AttributeError? That documents that Version is abstract. Not needed for coverage; skip or include? It could be brittle if the real module's Version defines parse. Let me check with get_info first.\n\nNow, imports: `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion, Version`. If `LooseVersion` doesn't exist, ImportError. Check via get_info.\n\nAlso, cleanup/state pollution: my tests don't mutate global state. The local subclass is module-level in the test file \u2014 that's fine, it's test-scoped. No monkeypatching needed if I use a subclass. But the prompt says \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test... use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since I avoid patching, nothing to clean. Maybe I'll avoid monkeypatch entirely. Or, alternatively, implement the NotImplemented coverage via monkeypatch on LooseVersion._cmp \u2014 but subclass is cleaner. I'll mentio